An accelerator with 80 GB of memory might fit those weights, but the remaining space must also support runtime state, intermediate data and other requirements. Training needs additional memory for items such as gradients and optimizer state. Weight size is not total memory demand.

Adding capacity could allow a larger workload to fit on one accelerator. That can change the need to distribute work across devices, creating a connection from HBM to networking. It does not guarantee faster execution if the workload already fits.

5. Bandwidth asks how fast data arrives

Suppose one stage of an invented workload must read 60 GB from HBM and has 3 TB/s of available bandwidth. Using decimal units, the transfer alone takes at least 0.02 seconds: 60 divided by 3,000.

At 6 TB/s, that ideal transfer time becomes 0.01 seconds. But that is not automatically a twofold improvement in the whole workload. Arithmetic, other transfers, communication and overhead may still dominate.

NVIDIA's roofline framework relates achievable math performance to both peak compute and memory bandwidth, using arithmetic intensity: operations performed per byte moved. We will study it more fully in the accelerator block.

6. Software can change the hardware requirement

Imagine software reusing data already close to the arithmetic units, reducing HBM traffic for the same mathematical task. A bandwidth-constrained calculation can improve without changing the memory hardware.

Now imagine a model growing enough to exceed available capacity. Even excellent data reuse does not by itself make the full working set fit.

The right question is therefore specific: is this workload limited by capacity, data movement, computation or something else? Calling every slow AI workload an HBM shortage confuses a runtime problem with a manufacturing shortage.

7. Return to the supply chain

Recall Lesson 031's fictional product requiring six HBM stacks per accelerator. If a redesign requires eight stacks and the supply remains 480,000 compatible stacks per month, the memory allocation supports 60,000 accelerators instead of 80,000.

That example holds stack output and compatibility fixed. Real designs may change memory density, stack height, interface and qualification requirements together.

More memory per accelerator can increase demand for memory, while also changing package area, power and cooling requirements. To assess the supplier opportunity, investigate the exact product and supply response rather than multiplying accelerator growth by a generic memory number.

Quick check — answer before reading on

A workload fits comfortably in memory but spends most of its time transferring data. Capacity doubles while usable bandwidth, software and compute remain unchanged. Should you predict a large speed improvement?

Answer: no. Extra space does not resolve the stated data-transfer limit. It could enable a different execution strategy, but that would be a separate assumption to test.

Remember these three things

- Capacity, bandwidth and latency answer different questions.

- Stacked memory and advanced packaging connect manufacturing choices to processor performance.

- Workload and software determine whether more memory capacity or more bandwidth is useful.

